From 4917775ae9c5af8a5499f00dc428be8f152ff242 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yu Watanabe <watanabe.yu+github@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 10:11:59 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] core: make /run/ honor ProtectSystem=strict again (#44736)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Since v248 (94293d65cd4125347e21b3e423d0e245226b1be2, "MountAPIVFS:
always mount a tmpfs on /run"), /run/ has been left writable under
ProtectSystem=strict whenever MountAPIVFS= is in effect — which is
implied by ProtectControlGroups=, ProtectKernelTunables=, ProtectProc=
and similar settings. MountAPIVFS= adds /run/ to the mount list, which
puts it on the deny-list of the recursive read-only remount, so /run/
escapes the read-only root. Worse, without a separate root directory
/run/ is the host's (the tmpfs is a no-op there), so this leaves the
*host's* /run/ writable from inside an otherwise strictly sandboxed
service.

Fix: only add the /run/ tmpfs to the MountAPIVFS= mounts when a separate
root directory (RootDirectory=/RootImage=) is used. There /run/ is
private and must stay writable (e.g. for /run/host/ and the os-release
staging). Without a separate root directory /run/ is no longer added to
the mount list, so it is covered by the read-only remount of
ProtectSystem=strict again. A writable /run/ (or a subdirectory) can
still be requested via ReadWritePaths= or RuntimeDirectory=. The /run/
tmpfs is now a plain MOUNT_TMPFS (always a fresh, private tmpfs), and
the special MOUNT_RUN mode is dropped.

Follow-ups for services that had relied on the writable /run/:
- hostnamed: restore ReadWritePaths=/run/systemd (writes
/run/systemd/fallback-hostname).
- nsresourced: add ReadWritePaths=/run/systemd (creates
/run/systemd/nsresource/registry).
- timedated: ignore failure to bind its Varlink socket directly
(normally socket-activated).
- networkd: tolerate -EROFS when binding the resolve-hook Varlink socket
(only reached on manual runs).

Tests:
- test-execute: ProtectSystem=yes/full/strict directory writability,
incl. /run/, and the ReadWritePaths= override.
- TEST-07-PID1: /run/ writability per ProtectSystem= mode.
- TEST-50-DISSECT: ProtectSystem=strict does not leak the host's /run/
with RootImage=.

Fixes a regression introduced in
94293d65cd4125347e21b3e423d0e245226b1be2 (v248).

Backport notes (v255 adaptation):
- Applies the namespace.c fix, the systemd.exec(5) documentation update and
  the new test-execute ProtectSystem= tests to the 255.2 source tree.
- Dropped hunks that do not apply to v255: the NEWS entry (v263 release
  notes), the timedated Varlink fallback (no Varlink support in v255
  timedated), units/systemd-nsresourced.service.in (unit added in v256),
  the test/units/ TEST-07/45/50/71/73 updates (new integration test layout
  introduced after v255) and the hostnamed ReadWritePaths= change (already
  present in v255).
- setup_namespace() gates the /run/ tmpfs on p->root_image ||
  p->root_directory, the v255 equivalent of upstream's
  pinned_resource_is_set(p->rootfs).
- test-execute: ASSERT_OK() does not exist in v255, use assert_se().


diff --git a/man/systemd.exec.xml b/man/systemd.exec.xml
index ca01ac9..439ee90 100644
--- a/man/systemd.exec.xml
+++ b/man/systemd.exec.xml
@@ -349,7 +349,13 @@
         already mounted. Note that this option has no effect unless used in conjunction with
         <varname>RootDirectory=</varname>/<varname>RootImage=</varname> as these four mounts are
         generally mounted in the host anyway, and unless the root directory is changed, the private mount namespace
-        will be a 1:1 copy of the host's, and include these four mounts. Note that the <filename>/dev/</filename> file
+        will be a 1:1 copy of the host's, and include these four mounts. A writable <filename>/run/</filename>
+        <literal>tmpfs</literal> is only set up when a separate root directory
+        (<varname>RootDirectory=</varname>/<varname>RootImage=</varname>) is used, where
+        <filename>/run/</filename> is private. Otherwise <filename>/run/</filename> is the host's and, under
+        <varname>ProtectSystem=strict</varname>, read-only like the rest of the hierarchy; request a writable
+        <filename>/run/</filename> (or a subdirectory of it) explicitly via <varname>ReadWritePaths=</varname>
+        or <varname>RuntimeDirectory=</varname> if needed. Note that the <filename>/dev/</filename> file
         system of the host is bind mounted if this option is used without <varname>PrivateDevices=</varname>. To run
         the service with a private, minimal version of <filename>/dev/</filename>, combine this option with
         <varname>PrivateDevices=</varname>.</para>
diff --git a/src/core/namespace.c b/src/core/namespace.c
index 175889e..a779e05 100644
--- a/src/core/namespace.c
+++ b/src/core/namespace.c
@@ -70,7 +70,6 @@ typedef enum MountMode {
         MOUNT_NOEXEC,
         MOUNT_EXEC,
         MOUNT_TMPFS,
-        MOUNT_RUN,
         MOUNT_EXTENSION_DIRECTORY, /* Bind-mounted outside the root directory, and used by subsequent mounts */
         MOUNT_EXTENSION_IMAGE,     /* Mounted outside the root directory, and used by subsequent mounts */
         MOUNT_MQUEUEFS,
@@ -115,13 +114,21 @@ typedef struct MountList {
         size_t n_mounts;
 } MountList;
 
-/* If MountAPIVFS= is used, let's mount /sys, /proc, /dev and /run into the it, but only as a fallback if the user hasn't mounted
- * something there already. These mounts are hence overridden by any other explicitly configured mounts. */
+/* If MountAPIVFS= is used, let's mount /proc/, /dev/ and /sys/, but only as a fallback if the user hasn't
+ * mounted something already. These mounts are hence overridden by any other explicitly configured mounts. */
 static const MountEntry apivfs_table[] = {
         { "/proc",               MOUNT_PROCFS,       false },
         { "/dev",                MOUNT_BIND_DEV,     false },
         { "/sys",                MOUNT_BIND_SYSFS,   false },
-        { "/run",                MOUNT_RUN,          false, .options_const = "mode=0755" TMPFS_LIMITS_RUN, .flags = MS_NOSUID|MS_NODEV|MS_STRICTATIME },
+};
+
+/* The writable /run/ tmpfs of MountAPIVFS=. Only set up when a separate root directory is used: there /run/
+ * is a private tmpfs that must be writable (e.g. for /run/host/). Without a separate root directory /run/ is
+ * the host's, and left out here so that it stays subject to the read-only remount of ProtectSystem=strict
+ * (and does not leak as writable). A fresh tmpfs is always mounted here, so that even a /run/ already present
+ * in the root tree (e.g. the host's bind-mounted in) is replaced by a private one. */
+static const MountEntry apivfs_run_table[] = {
+        { "/run",                MOUNT_TMPFS,        false, .options_const = "mode=0755" TMPFS_LIMITS_RUN, .flags = MS_NOSUID|MS_NODEV|MS_STRICTATIME },
 };
 
 /* ProtectKernelTunables= option and the related filesystem APIs */
@@ -244,7 +251,6 @@ static const char * const mount_mode_table[_MOUNT_MODE_MAX] = {
         [MOUNT_NOEXEC]                = "noexec",
         [MOUNT_EXEC]                  = "exec",
         [MOUNT_TMPFS]                 = "tmpfs",
-        [MOUNT_RUN]                   = "run",
         [MOUNT_EXTENSION_DIRECTORY]   = "extension-directory",
         [MOUNT_EXTENSION_IMAGE]       = "extension-image",
         [MOUNT_MQUEUEFS]              = "mqueuefs",
@@ -1295,20 +1301,6 @@ static int mount_tmpfs(const MountEntry *m) {
         return 1;
 }
 
-static int mount_run(const MountEntry *m) {
-        int r;
-
-        assert(m);
-
-        r = path_is_mount_point(mount_entry_path(m), NULL, 0);
-        if (r < 0 && r != -ENOENT)
-                return log_debug_errno(r, "Unable to determine whether /run is already mounted: %m");
-        if (r > 0) /* make this a NOP if /run is already a mount point */
-                return 0;
-
-        return mount_tmpfs(m);
-}
-
 static int mount_mqueuefs(const MountEntry *m) {
         int r;
         const char *entry_path;
@@ -1653,9 +1645,6 @@ static int apply_one_mount(
         case MOUNT_PROCFS:
                 return mount_procfs(m, p);
 
-        case MOUNT_RUN:
-                return mount_run(m);
-
         case MOUNT_MQUEUEFS:
                 return mount_mqueuefs(m);
 
@@ -2337,6 +2326,19 @@ int setup_namespace(const NamespaceParameters *p, char **error_path) {
                                          p->ignore_protect_paths);
                 if (r < 0)
                         return r;
+
+                /* Only set up a writable /run/ tmpfs when a separate root directory is used. Without one, /run/
+                 * is the host's; leaving it out here keeps it subject to the read-only remount of
+                 * ProtectSystem=strict (and avoids leaking the host's /run/ as writable). A writable /run/ (or
+                 * a subdirectory of it) can still be requested via ReadWritePaths= or RuntimeDirectory=. */
+                if (p->root_image || p->root_directory) {
+                        r = append_static_mounts(&ml,
+                                                 apivfs_run_table,
+                                                 ELEMENTSOF(apivfs_run_table),
+                                                 p->ignore_protect_paths);
+                        if (r < 0)
+                                return r;
+                }
         }
 
         /* Note, if proc is mounted with subset=pid then neither of the two paths will exist, i.e. they are
diff --git a/src/test/test-execute.c b/src/test/test-execute.c
index 43b1254..91f0ea5 100644
--- a/src/test/test-execute.c
+++ b/src/test/test-execute.c
@@ -529,6 +529,33 @@ static void test_exec_readwritepaths(Manager *m) {
         test(m, "exec-readwritepaths-mount-propagation.service", can_unshare ? 0 : MANAGER_IS_SYSTEM(m) ? EXIT_FAILURE : EXIT_NAMESPACE, CLD_EXITED);
 }
 
+static void test_exec_protectsystem(Manager *m) {
+
+        /* ProtectSystem= has no effect (and does not fail) when mount namespacing is unavailable, so these
+         * tests only make sense when we can actually set up namespaces. */
+        if (!can_unshare) {
+                log_notice("Cannot use namespaces, skipping %s", __func__);
+                return;
+        }
+
+        /* ProtectSystem=yes makes /usr/ read-only, but leaves /etc/ and /run/ writable. The /etc/ check
+         * writes to the host's /etc/, so only run it when that is writable. */
+        if (path_is_read_only_fs("/etc") == 0)
+                test(m, "exec-protectsystem-yes.service", 0, CLD_EXITED);
+
+        /* ProtectSystem=full additionally makes /etc/ read-only; /run/ stays writable. */
+        test(m, "exec-protectsystem-full.service", 0, CLD_EXITED);
+
+        /* ProtectSystem=strict makes everything read-only, including /run/ (even with the MountAPIVFS=
+         * tmpfs) ... */
+        test(m, "exec-protectsystem-strict.service", 0, CLD_EXITED);
+        /* ... but ReadWritePaths= can still make a subdirectory of /run/ writable. The target has to exist
+         * for ReadWritePaths= to bind it, so create it on the host first. */
+        assert_se(mkdir_p("/run/test-exec-run-readwrite", 0755) >= 0);
+        test(m, "exec-protectsystem-strict-readwrite.service", 0, CLD_EXITED);
+        (void) rm_rf("/run/test-exec-run-readwrite", REMOVE_ROOT|REMOVE_PHYSICAL);
+}
+
 static void test_exec_inaccessiblepaths(Manager *m) {
 
         if (!is_inaccessible_available()) {
@@ -1251,6 +1278,7 @@ static void run_tests(RuntimeScope scope, char **patterns) {
                 entry(test_exec_privatetmp),
                 entry(test_exec_protecthome),
                 entry(test_exec_protectkernelmodules),
+                entry(test_exec_protectsystem),
                 entry(test_exec_readonlypaths),
                 entry(test_exec_readwritepaths),
                 entry(test_exec_restrictnamespaces),
diff --git a/test/test-execute/exec-protectsystem-full.service b/test/test-execute/exec-protectsystem-full.service
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8172c69
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/test-execute/exec-protectsystem-full.service
@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-or-later
+[Unit]
+Description=Test ProtectSystem=full
+
+[Service]
+ProtectSystem=full
+Type=oneshot
+# /usr/ and /etc/ are read-only, while /run/ stays writable.
+ExecStart=bash -x -c '! touch /usr/thisshouldnotwork'
+ExecStart=bash -x -c '! touch /etc/thisshouldnotwork'
+ExecStart=bash -x -c 'touch /run/.test-exec-protectsystem-full && rm /run/.test-exec-protectsystem-full'
diff --git a/test/test-execute/exec-protectsystem-strict-readwrite.service b/test/test-execute/exec-protectsystem-strict-readwrite.service
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..0489514
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/test-execute/exec-protectsystem-strict-readwrite.service
@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-or-later
+[Unit]
+Description=Test that ReadWritePaths= overrides the read-only /run/ of ProtectSystem=strict
+
+[Service]
+ProtectSystem=strict
+MountAPIVFS=yes
+# The target is created on the host by the test harness beforehand.
+ReadWritePaths=/run/test-exec-run-readwrite
+Type=oneshot
+# /run/ itself stays read-only, but the ReadWritePaths= subdirectory is writable.
+ExecStart=bash -x -c '! touch /run/thisshouldnotwork && touch /run/test-exec-run-readwrite/thisworks'
diff --git a/test/test-execute/exec-protectsystem-strict.service b/test/test-execute/exec-protectsystem-strict.service
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..09a5542
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/test-execute/exec-protectsystem-strict.service
@@ -0,0 +1,14 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-or-later
+[Unit]
+Description=Test ProtectSystem=strict
+
+[Service]
+ProtectSystem=strict
+MountAPIVFS=yes
+Type=oneshot
+# The whole hierarchy is read-only, including /run/ (even though MountAPIVFS= would otherwise set up a
+# writable tmpfs there).
+ExecStart=bash -x -c '! touch /thisshouldnotwork'
+ExecStart=bash -x -c '! touch /usr/thisshouldnotwork'
+ExecStart=bash -x -c '! touch /etc/thisshouldnotwork'
+ExecStart=bash -x -c '! touch /run/thisshouldnotwork'
diff --git a/test/test-execute/exec-protectsystem-yes.service b/test/test-execute/exec-protectsystem-yes.service
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4fa1d0e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/test-execute/exec-protectsystem-yes.service
